<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>ecology</title></head><body><h1>ecology</h1><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li>Ecology — Concepts and Ecosystems</li><li>Ecosystem ecology: Concept of ecology, biotic and abiotic…</li><li>Ecosystem Ecology</li><li>Concept of ecosystem, structural and functional aspects o…</li><li>Food Chain, Food Web and Ecological Pyramids</li><li>Food Chain and Food Web</li><li>Biogeochemical Cycles</li><li>Biogeochemical cycles: carbon and nitrogen cycles; concep…</li><li>Ecological Adaptations — Hydrophytes and Xerophytes</li><li>Ecological Adaptation</li><li>Ecological Imbalances — Pollution and Climate Change</li><li>Ecological Imbalances</li><li>Ecological imbalances: Greenhouse effects and climate cha…</li></ul><h2>Ecology — Concepts and Ecosystems</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Detailed notes on 01 ecosystem ecology concept of ecology biotic and abiotic factors species interactions concept of ecosystem structural and functional aspects of pond and forest ecosystem within the ecology unit.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Ecology: the study of the interactions between organisms and their environment (biotic + abiotic).</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Biotic factors: living components (plants, animals, microorganisms); Abiotic factors: non-living (light, temperature, water, soil, pH, gases).</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Species interactions: mutualism (+/+), commensalism (+/0), parasitism (+/-), predation (+/-), competition (-/-), amensalism (-/0), neutralism (0/0).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Ecosystem: a functional unit of biotic + abiotic components interacting; types: aquatic (pond, lake, ocean) and terrestrial (grassland, forest, desert).</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Structural aspects: producers (autotrophs), consumers (herbivores, carnivores, omnivores), decomposers (bacteria, fungi); trophic levels.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Functional aspects: energy flow (unidirectional, sun → producers → consumers → heat), nutrient cycling (biogeochemical), and food chains/webs.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Pond and forest ecosystems: producers (phytoplankton / trees), consumers (zooplankton / herbivores / carnivores), decomposers; abiotic factors (light, temperature, water, soil).</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Nepal: Terai, hills, and mountains each have distinct ecosystems (e.g., Chitwan Terai, temperate oak-rhododendron hills, alpine zone).</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>NEB focus: concept of ecology, biotic/abiotic factors, species interactions, and structural + functional aspects of pond and forest ecosystems.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>In nature an organism does not exist in isolation, it is associated with organisms of its own and of different kinds as well as with the physical environment.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>If the organism is plant, it depends for growth on the aerial environment for light, temperature and carbon dioxide, on the soil environment for inorganic nutrients and water.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>If it is an animal it depends on the plants for food and shelter.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>If it is a microorganism it depend on the dead organic matter from plants and animals for its food, and releases the nutrients tied in the organic matter for the reuse of plants.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Thus, there are continuous interactions among the organisms and between the organisms and the habitat resulting into an integrated, functional unit.</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>This functional unit is known as ecosystem.</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>It is the structural and functional unit of ecology.</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>Thus, ecosystem is defined as ‘the structural and functional unit of ecology where the living organisms interact with each other and the surrounding environment’.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Ecology: study of organism-environment interactions.</td></tr><tr><td>Biotic (living) vs abiotic (non-living) factors.</td></tr><tr><td>Species interactions: mutualism, commensalism, parasitism, predation, competition.</td></tr><tr><td>Ecosystem = biotic + abiotic; types: aquatic + terrestrial.</td></tr><tr><td>Structural: producers, consumers, decomposers; trophic levels.</td></tr><tr><td>Functional: energy flow (unidirectional) + nutrient cycling (biogeochemical).</td></tr><tr><td>Pond and forest ecosystems: Nepal examples.</td></tr><tr><td>In nature an organism does not exist in isolation, it is associated with organisms of its own and of different kinds as well as with the physical environment.</td></tr><tr><td>If the organism is plant, it depends for growth on the aerial environment for light, temperature and carbon dioxide, on the soil environment for inorganic nutrients and water.</td></tr><tr><td>If it is an animal it depends on the plants for food and shelter.</td></tr><tr><td>If it is a microorganism it depend on the dead organic matter from plants and animals for its food, and releases the nutrients tied in the organic matter for the reuse of plants.</td></tr><tr><td>Thus, there are continuous interactions among the organisms and between the organisms and the habitat resulting into an integrated, functional unit.</td></tr><tr><td>• Ecosystem ecology</td></tr><tr><td>• Ecology</td></tr><tr><td>• Biotic</td></tr><tr><td>• Abiotic factors</td></tr><tr><td>• Species interactions</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ Misconception about 01 ecosystem ecology concept of ecology biotic and abiotic factors species interactions concept of ecosystem structural and functional aspects of pond and forest ecosystem. ✅ Scientific explanation.</td></tr><tr><td>Predation (+/-) and parasitism (+/-) both harm one partner; predation usually KILLS the prey quickly, parasitism is long-term and usually does not kill the host immediately.</td></tr><tr><td>Mutualism (+/+) benefits both; commensalism (+/0) benefits one, affects neither; parasitism (+/-) benefits one, harms the other.</td></tr><tr><td>Competition (-/-) harms BOTH species (interspecific); intra-specific competition is within the same species.</td></tr><tr><td>An ecosystem is a FUNCTIONAL unit (energy flow + nutrient cycling); a community is just the assemblage of species.</td></tr><tr><td>Energy flows ONEDIRECTIONALLY through an ecosystem (sun → producers → consumers → heat); it is NOT cycled.</td></tr><tr><td>Nutrients (C, N, P) ARE cycled (biogeochemical); energy is not cycled.</td></tr><tr><td>Distinguish between key concepts in Ecosystem Ecology.</td></tr><tr><td>Understand the differences between similar topics.</td></tr><tr><td>Practice identifying the correct application of principles.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Summary of 01 ecosystem ecology concept of ecology biotic and abiotic factors species interactions concept of ecosystem structural and functional aspects of pond and forest ecosystem. Ecology studies the interactions between organisms and their environment. Biotic (living) and abiotic (non-living) factors shape every ecosystem. Species interact through mutualism, commensalism, parasitism, predation, and competition. An ecosystem is a functional unit of biotic and abiotic components; its structural aspects (producers, consumers, decomposers, trophic levels) and functional aspects (unidirectional energy flow + cyclic nutrient cycling) define how it operates. Pond and forest ecosystems (e.g., Chitwan Terai, temperate hills) illustrate these principles in the Nepali context. In nature an organism does not exist in isolation,  it is associated with organisms of its
own and of different  kinds as well as with the physical environment. If the organism is plant,
it depends for growth on the aerial environment  for light, temperature and carbon dioxide, on
the soil environment for inorganic nutrients and water. If it is an animal it depends on the plants
for food and shelter. If it is a microorganism  it depend on the dead organic matter from plants
and animals  for Ecology studies the interactions between organisms and their environment. Biotic (living) and abiotic (non-living) factors shape every ecosystem. Species interact through mutualism, commensalism, parasitism, predation, and competition. An ecosystem is a functional unit of biotic and abiotic components; its structural aspects (producers, consumers, decomposers, trophic levels) and functional aspects (unidirectional energy flow + cyclic nutrient cycling) define how it operates. Pond and forest ecosystems (e.g., Chitwan Terai, temperate hills) illustrate these principles in the Nepali context.</p><hr><h2>Ecosystem ecology: Concept of ecology, biotic and abiotic…</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Scope.** Ecosystem ecology: Concept of ecology, biotic and abiotic… — Ecology, Biology (class-11-notes).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Tested.** define it · list: ecosystem ecology, ecology, biotic, abiotic factors · one worked example · one misconception each.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Ecology.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Biotic.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Abiotic factors.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Species interactions.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Ecosystem ecology</td></tr><tr><td>• Ecology</td></tr><tr><td>• Biotic</td></tr><tr><td>• Abiotic factors</td></tr><tr><td>• Species interactions</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ treat "Ecosystem ecology" as one blob → ✅ split into: ecosystem ecology, ecology, biotic.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ state the rule without its limit → ✅ note when it applies / stops applying.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ no diagram → ✅ add one labelled figure / structured list.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Ecosystem ecology: Concept of ecology, biotic and abiotic… (Ecology, Biology, class-11-notes) covers Ecosystem ecology, ecology, biotic. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Ecosystem Ecology</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Ecology: the study of the interactions between organisms and their environment (biotic + abiotic).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Biotic factors: living components (plants, animals, microorganisms); Abiotic factors: non-living (light, temperature, water, soil, pH, gases).</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Species interactions: mutualism (+/+), commensalism (+/0), parasitism (+/-), predation (+/-), competition (-/-), amensalism (-/0), neutralism (0/0).</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Ecosystem: a functional unit of biotic + abiotic components interacting; types: aquatic (pond, lake, ocean) and terrestrial (grassland, forest, desert).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Structural aspects: producers (autotrophs), consumers (herbivores, carnivores, omnivores), decomposers (bacteria, fungi); trophic levels.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Functional aspects: energy flow (unidirectional, sun → producers → consumers → heat), nutrient cycling (biogeochemical), and food chains/webs.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Pond and forest ecosystems: producers (phytoplankton / trees), consumers (zooplankton / herbivores / carnivores), decomposers; abiotic factors (light, temperature, water, soil).</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Nepal: Terai, hills, and mountains each have distinct ecosystems (e.g., Chitwan Terai, temperate oak-rhododendron hills, alpine zone).</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>NEB focus: concept of ecology, biotic/abiotic factors, species interactions, and structural + functional aspects of pond and forest ecosystems.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Ecology: study of organism-environment interactions.</td></tr><tr><td>Biotic (living) vs abiotic (non-living) factors.</td></tr><tr><td>Species interactions: mutualism, commensalism, parasitism, predation, competition.</td></tr><tr><td>Ecosystem = biotic + abiotic; types: aquatic + terrestrial.</td></tr><tr><td>Structural: producers, consumers, decomposers; trophic levels.</td></tr><tr><td>Functional: energy flow (unidirectional) + nutrient cycling (biogeochemical).</td></tr><tr><td>Pond and forest ecosystems: Nepal examples.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Predation (+/-) and parasitism (+/-) both harm one partner; predation usually KILLS the prey quickly, parasitism is long-term and usually does not kill the host immediately.</td></tr><tr><td>Mutualism (+/+) benefits both; commensalism (+/0) benefits one, affects neither; parasitism (+/-) benefits one, harms the other.</td></tr><tr><td>Competition (-/-) harms BOTH species (interspecific); intra-specific competition is within the same species.</td></tr><tr><td>An ecosystem is a FUNCTIONAL unit (energy flow + nutrient cycling); a community is just the assemblage of species.</td></tr><tr><td>Energy flows ONEDIRECTIONALLY through an ecosystem (sun → producers → consumers → heat); it is NOT cycled.</td></tr><tr><td>Nutrients (C, N, P) ARE cycled (biogeochemical); energy is not cycled.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Ecology studies the interactions between organisms and their environment. Biotic (living) and abiotic (non-living) factors shape every ecosystem. Species interact through mutualism, commensalism, parasitism, predation, and competition. An ecosystem is a functional unit of biotic and abiotic components; its structural aspects (producers, consumers, decomposers, trophic levels) and functional aspects (unidirectional energy flow + cyclic nutrient cycling) define how it operates. Pond and forest ecosystems (e.g., Chitwan Terai, temperate hills) illustrate these principles in the Nepali context.</p><hr><h2>Concept of ecosystem, structural and functional aspects o…</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Scope.** Concept of ecosystem, structural and functional aspects o… — Ecology, Biology (class-11-notes).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Tested.** define it · list: ecosystem, structural, functional aspects of pond, forest ecosystem · one worked example · one misconception each.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Structural.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Functional aspects of pond.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Forest ecosystem.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Ecosystem</td></tr><tr><td>• Structural</td></tr><tr><td>• Functional aspects of pond</td></tr><tr><td>• Forest ecosystem</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ treat "ecosystem" as one blob → ✅ split into: ecosystem, structural, functional aspects of pond.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ state the rule without its limit → ✅ note when it applies / stops applying.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ no diagram → ✅ add one labelled figure / structured list.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Concept of ecosystem, structural and functional aspects o… (Ecology, Biology, class-11-notes) covers ecosystem, structural, functional aspects of pond. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Food Chain, Food Web and Ecological Pyramids</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Detailed notes on 02 food chain food web trophic level ecological pyramids productivity within the ecology unit.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Food chain: a linear sequence of organisms where each is eaten by the next; transfers energy and matter (sun → producer → primary consumer → secondary consumer → tertiary consumer).</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Food web: a network of interconnected food chains; more realistic than a single chain; shows multiple feeding relationships.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Trophic levels: TL1 producers (autotrophs), TL2 primary consumers (herbivores), TL3 secondary consumers (carnivores), TL4+ tertiary consumers.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Ecological pyramids: number (pyramid of numbers), biomass (pyramid of biomass), energy (pyramid of energy).</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Pyramid of energy: always upright; each level has less energy than the one below (10% rule).</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Pyramid of numbers: usually upright; can be inverted (e.g., one tree supports many insects).</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Pyramid of biomass: usually upright; can be inverted in aquatic ecosystems (phytoplankton turn over fast).</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Productivity: gross primary productivity (GPP = total photosynthesis), net primary productivity (NPP = GPP − plant respiration).</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Consumer productivity: energy assimilated by consumers per unit time.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>NEB focus: food chain, food web, trophic levels, ecological pyramids, and productivity.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>In an ecosystem one can observe the transfer or flow of energy from one trophic level to other trophic level by the process of eating and being eaten.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>It forms a linear chain called food chain.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>There are basically three types of food chain in nature: 1.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Grazing food chain: The food chain that starts from the green plants and ends to carnivores (top consumer) by passing though herbivores.</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>Detritus food chain: The organic wastes and dead matter derived from grazing food chain are called as detritus.</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>The food chain that starts with detritus is called detritus food chain.</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>Parasitic food chain: This food chain starts either from producers or consumers.</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>It goes from large organisms to smaller ones without killing.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Food chain</td></tr><tr><td>• Food web</td></tr><tr><td>• Trophic level</td></tr><tr><td>• Ecological pyramids</td></tr><tr><td>• Productivity</td></tr><tr><td>Food chain = linear; food web = network of chains.</td></tr><tr><td>Trophic levels: TL1 producers, TL2 herbivores, TL3 carnivores, TL4+ top carnivores.</td></tr><tr><td>Three pyramids: number, biomass, energy.</td></tr><tr><td>Pyramid of energy is ALWAYS upright; number and biomass can be inverted.</td></tr><tr><td>10% rule: ~10% energy transfers between levels.</td></tr><tr><td>Productivity: GPP (total), NPP (GPP − respiration).</td></tr><tr><td>NPP is the energy available to herbivores.</td></tr><tr><td>In an ecosystem one can observe the transfer or flow of energy from one trophic level to other trophic level by the process of eating and being eaten.</td></tr><tr><td>It forms a linear chain called food chain.</td></tr><tr><td>There are basically three types of food chain in nature: 1.</td></tr><tr><td>Grazing food chain: The food chain that starts from the green plants and ends to carnivores (top consumer) by passing though herbivores.</td></tr><tr><td>Detritus food chain: The organic wastes and dead matter derived from grazing food chain are called as detritus.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ Misconception about 02 food chain food web trophic level ecological pyramids productivity. ✅ Scientific explanation.</td></tr><tr><td>A food chain is LINEAR; a food web is a NETWORK of chains (more realistic).</td></tr><tr><td>Producers (plants, phytoplankton) are at trophic level 1; herbivores at level 2; carnivores at level 3+.</td></tr><tr><td>The pyramid of ENERGY is always upright; the pyramids of numbers and biomass CAN be inverted.</td></tr><tr><td>An inverted pyramid of numbers occurs when one large organism supports many small ones (e.g., a tree with many insects).</td></tr><tr><td>An inverted pyramid of biomass occurs in aquatic ecosystems where phytoplankton (producers) have low standing biomass but high turnover.</td></tr><tr><td>The 10% rule: only ~10% of energy transfers from one trophic level to the next; ~90% is lost as heat.</td></tr><tr><td>Distinguish between key concepts in Food Chain and Food Web.</td></tr><tr><td>Understand the differences between similar topics.</td></tr><tr><td>Practice identifying the correct application of principles.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Summary of 02 food chain food web trophic level ecological pyramids productivity. Food chain, food web, trophic level, ecological pyramids,… (Ecology, Biology, class-11-notes) covers Food chain, food web, trophic level. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these. Energy and matter flow through ecosystems in food chains (linear) and food webs (networks). Trophic levels (producers → primary consumers → secondary → tertiary) structure these. Ecological pyramids (number, biomass, energy) visualise trophic structure; the pyramid of energy is always upright because ~90% of energy is lost at each transfer (10% rule). Productivity (GPP, NPP) measures the rate of energy capture and availability to consumers. NEB focuses on food chains, food webs, trophic levels, the three pyramids, and productivity calculations. In an ecosystem one can observe the transfer or flow of energy from one trophic level to other
trophic level by the process of eating and being eaten. It forms a linear chain called food chain.
There are basically three types of food chain in nature:
1. Grazing food chain:
The food chain  that  starts  from  the green  plants  and ends  to carnivores  (top  consumer)  by
passing though herbivores.

Figure: 4.1.3 Grazing food chain
2. Detritus food chain:
The organic wastes and dead</p><hr><h2>Food Chain and Food Web</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Food chain: a linear sequence of organisms where each is eaten by the next; transfers energy and matter (sun → producer → primary consumer → secondary consumer → tertiary consumer).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Food web: a network of interconnected food chains; more realistic than a single chain; shows multiple feeding relationships.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Trophic levels: TL1 producers (autotrophs), TL2 primary consumers (herbivores), TL3 secondary consumers (carnivores), TL4+ tertiary consumers.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Ecological pyramids: number (pyramid of numbers), biomass (pyramid of biomass), energy (pyramid of energy).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Pyramid of energy: always upright; each level has less energy than the one below (10% rule).</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Pyramid of numbers: usually upright; can be inverted (e.g., one tree supports many insects).</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Pyramid of biomass: usually upright; can be inverted in aquatic ecosystems (phytoplankton turn over fast).</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Productivity: gross primary productivity (GPP = total photosynthesis), net primary productivity (NPP = GPP − plant respiration).</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Consumer productivity: energy assimilated by consumers per unit time.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>NEB focus: food chain, food web, trophic levels, ecological pyramids, and productivity.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Food chain = linear; food web = network of chains.</td></tr><tr><td>Trophic levels: TL1 producers, TL2 herbivores, TL3 carnivores, TL4+ top carnivores.</td></tr><tr><td>Three pyramids: number, biomass, energy.</td></tr><tr><td>Pyramid of energy is ALWAYS upright; number and biomass can be inverted.</td></tr><tr><td>10% rule: ~10% energy transfers between levels.</td></tr><tr><td>Productivity: GPP (total), NPP (GPP − respiration).</td></tr><tr><td>NPP is the energy available to herbivores.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>A food chain is LINEAR; a food web is a NETWORK of chains (more realistic).</td></tr><tr><td>Producers (plants, phytoplankton) are at trophic level 1; herbivores at level 2; carnivores at level 3+.</td></tr><tr><td>The pyramid of ENERGY is always upright; the pyramids of numbers and biomass CAN be inverted.</td></tr><tr><td>An inverted pyramid of numbers occurs when one large organism supports many small ones (e.g., a tree with many insects).</td></tr><tr><td>An inverted pyramid of biomass occurs in aquatic ecosystems where phytoplankton (producers) have low standing biomass but high turnover.</td></tr><tr><td>The 10% rule: only ~10% of energy transfers from one trophic level to the next; ~90% is lost as heat.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Energy and matter flow through ecosystems in food chains (linear) and food webs (networks). Trophic levels (producers → primary consumers → secondary → tertiary) structure these. Ecological pyramids (number, biomass, energy) visualise trophic structure; the pyramid of energy is always upright because ~90% of energy is lost at each transfer (10% rule). Productivity (GPP, NPP) measures the rate of energy capture and availability to consumers. NEB focuses on food chains, food webs, trophic levels, the three pyramids, and productivity calculations.</p><hr><h2>Biogeochemical Cycles</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Biogeochemical cycles: the movement of elements (C, N, P, H, O) through biotic (living) and abiotic (non-living) compartments.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Carbon cycle: atmospheric CO2 → photosynthesis (plants) → respiration (animals + plants) → decomposition → soil → oceans; combustion (fossil fuels) releases CO2; carbonate rocks (limestone) store carbon long-term.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Nitrogen cycle: atmospheric N2 (78%) → nitrogen fixation (Rhizobium, Azotobacter, lightning) → ammonia (NH3) → nitrification (Nitrosomonas → nitrite; Nitrobacter → nitrate) → assimilation (plants) → ammonification (decomposers) → denitrification (Pseudomonas → N2).</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Phosphorus cycle: rock (apatite) → weathering → soil → plants → animals → decomposition → sediment; no gaseous phase (unlike C, N).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Water cycle: evaporation → condensation → precipitation → runoff → oceans.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Succession: the process by which an ecosystem changes over time; primary (on bare rock, no soil) vs secondary (on existing soil).</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Primary succession: bare rock → lichens/mosses → grasses → shrubs → forest (climax).</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Secondary succession: after a disturbance (fire, flood, logging) → grasses → shrubs → forest (faster than primary).</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Climax community: stable, mature community in equilibrium with the environment (e.g., mature oak-rhododendron forest in Nepal).</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>NEB focus: carbon and nitrogen cycles; concept of succession.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Biogeochemical cycles: C, N, P, H, O move through biotic + abiotic compartments.</td></tr><tr><td>Carbon cycle: gaseous (CO2); photosynthesis, respiration, decomposition, combustion.</td></tr><tr><td>Nitrogen cycle: N2 → NH3 → NO2- → NO3- → assimilation → ammonification → N2.</td></tr><tr><td>Bacteria: Rhizobium (fixation), Nitrosomonas (nitrification), Pseudomonas (denitrification).</td></tr><tr><td>Phosphorus cycle: no gaseous phase; slowest cycle.</td></tr><tr><td>Succession: primary (bare rock) vs secondary (existing soil); climax community is stable.</td></tr><tr><td>Climax: mature, stable community in equilibrium with the environment.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Nitrogen fixation converts N2 to ammonia (NH3/NH4+); nitrification converts ammonia to nitrite then nitrate; denitrification converts nitrate back to N2.</td></tr><tr><td>Rhizobium (symbiotic, in legume root nodules) and Azotobacter (free-living, in soil) are both nitrogen-fixing bacteria.</td></tr><tr><td>The carbon cycle has a gaseous phase (CO2); the phosphorus cycle does NOT have a gaseous phase.</td></tr><tr><td>Primary succession starts on BARE ROCK (no soil); secondary succession starts on EXISTING SOIL (after disturbance).</td></tr><tr><td>Succession is a PREDICTABLE, directional process toward a stable climax community.</td></tr><tr><td>Decomposition releases nutrients (C, N, P) back into the soil; without it, cycles would stop.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Biogeochemical cycles move essential elements through living and non-living compartments. The carbon cycle (gaseous CO2) and the nitrogen cycle (N2 → NH3 → NO2- → NO3- → N2) are the most important; bacteria (Rhizobium, Nitrosomonas, Pseudomonas) drive key steps. The phosphorus cycle has no gaseous phase and is the slowest. Succession is the directional change of an ecosystem toward a stable climax community: primary succession starts on bare rock, secondary on existing soil. NEB focuses on the carbon and nitrogen cycles and the concept of succession.</p><hr><h2>Biogeochemical cycles: carbon and nitrogen cycles; concep…</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Fact.** Force on moving charge F = q(v×B); on a wire F = BIL sinθ.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Fact.** Faraday: EMF ε = −dΦ/dt; Lenz's law gives the sign (induced current opposes the change in flux).</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Fact.** Self-inductance L = NΦ/I; inductance stores energy U = ½LI². Mutual inductance M couples two coils.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Detailed notes on 03 biogeochemical cycles carbon and nitrogen cycles concept of succession within the ecology unit.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Biogeochemical cycles: the movement of elements (C, N, P, H, O) through biotic (living) and abiotic (non-living) compartments.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Carbon cycle: atmospheric CO2 → photosynthesis (plants) → respiration (animals + plants) → decomposition → soil → oceans; combustion (fossil fuels) releases CO2; carbonate rocks (limestone) store carbon long-term.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Nitrogen cycle: atmospheric N2 (78%) → nitrogen fixation (Rhizobium, Azotobacter, lightning) → ammonia (NH3) → nitrification (Nitrosomonas → nitrite; Nitrobacter → nitrate) → assimilation (plants) → ammonification (decomposers) → denitrification (Pseudomonas → N2).</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Phosphorus cycle: rock (apatite) → weathering → soil → plants → animals → decomposition → sediment; no gaseous phase (unlike C, N).</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Water cycle: evaporation → condensation → precipitation → runoff → oceans.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Succession: the process by which an ecosystem changes over time; primary (on bare rock, no soil) vs secondary (on existing soil).</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Primary succession: bare rock → lichens/mosses → grasses → shrubs → forest (climax).</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Secondary succession: after a disturbance (fire, flood, logging) → grasses → shrubs → forest (faster than primary).</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Climax community: stable, mature community in equilibrium with the environment (e.g., mature oak-rhododendron forest in Nepal).</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>NEB focus: carbon and nitrogen cycles; concept of succession.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>You have already learnt that living organisms require several chemical elements for their life process.</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>They may be used as part of their structural component or as parts of enzymes which influence various life processes.</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>Nutrients are continuously exchanged between the organism and their physical environment.</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>The cyclic movement of inorganic nutrients or essential elements between abiotic environment and biotic communities is called biogeochemical cycle.</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>Biogeochemical cycle is of three types: hydrological cycle (water cycle), gaseous cycles (e.</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>Carbon cycle, oxygen cycle, nitrogen cycle etc.</td></tr><tr><td>21</td><td>) and sedimentary cycle (like, phosphorous cycle, calcium cycle, sulphur cycle, etc.</td></tr><tr><td>22</td><td>Te rtiary Cons ume r Se condary Cons ume r Primary Cons umer Produce r.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Biogeochemical cycles</td></tr><tr><td>• Carbon</td></tr><tr><td>• Nitrogen cycles</td></tr><tr><td>• Concept of succession</td></tr><tr><td>Biogeochemical cycles: C, N, P, H, O move through biotic + abiotic compartments.</td></tr><tr><td>Carbon cycle: gaseous (CO2); photosynthesis, respiration, decomposition, combustion.</td></tr><tr><td>Nitrogen cycle: N2 → NH3 → NO2- → NO3- → assimilation → ammonification → N2.</td></tr><tr><td>Bacteria: Rhizobium (fixation), Nitrosomonas (nitrification), Pseudomonas (denitrification).</td></tr><tr><td>Phosphorus cycle: no gaseous phase; slowest cycle.</td></tr><tr><td>Succession: primary (bare rock) vs secondary (existing soil); climax community is stable.</td></tr><tr><td>Climax: mature, stable community in equilibrium with the environment.</td></tr><tr><td>You have already learnt that living organisms require several chemical elements for their life process.</td></tr><tr><td>They may be used as part of their structural component or as parts of enzymes which influence various life processes.</td></tr><tr><td>Nutrients are continuously exchanged between the organism and their physical environment.</td></tr><tr><td>The cyclic movement of inorganic nutrients or essential elements between abiotic environment and biotic communities is called biogeochemical cycle.</td></tr><tr><td>Biogeochemical cycle is of three types: hydrological cycle (water cycle), gaseous cycles (e.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ skip the key fact → ✅ force on moving charge f = q(v×b); on a wire f = bil sinθ..</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Misconception about 03 biogeochemical cycles carbon and nitrogen cycles concept of succession. ✅ Scientific explanation.</td></tr><tr><td>Nitrogen fixation converts N2 to ammonia (NH3/NH4+); nitrification converts ammonia to nitrite then nitrate; denitrification converts nitrate back to N2.</td></tr><tr><td>Rhizobium (symbiotic, in legume root nodules) and Azotobacter (free-living, in soil) are both nitrogen-fixing bacteria.</td></tr><tr><td>The carbon cycle has a gaseous phase (CO2); the phosphorus cycle does NOT have a gaseous phase.</td></tr><tr><td>Primary succession starts on BARE ROCK (no soil); secondary succession starts on EXISTING SOIL (after disturbance).</td></tr><tr><td>Succession is a PREDICTABLE, directional process toward a stable climax community.</td></tr><tr><td>Decomposition releases nutrients (C, N, P) back into the soil; without it, cycles would stop.</td></tr><tr><td>Distinguish between key concepts in Biogeochemical Cycles.</td></tr><tr><td>Understand the differences between similar topics.</td></tr><tr><td>Practice identifying the correct application of principles.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Biogeochemical cycles: carbon and nitrogen cycles; concep… (Ecology, Biology, class-11-notes) covers Biogeochemical cycles, carbon, nitrogen cycles. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these. Summary of 03 biogeochemical cycles carbon and nitrogen cycles concept of succession. Biogeochemical cycles move essential elements through living and non-living compartments. The carbon cycle (gaseous CO2) and the nitrogen cycle (N2 → NH3 → NO2- → NO3- → N2) are the most important; bacteria (Rhizobium, Nitrosomonas, Pseudomonas) drive key steps. The phosphorus cycle has no gaseous phase and is the slowest. Succession is the directional change of an ecosystem toward a stable climax community: primary succession starts on bare rock, secondary on existing soil. NEB focuses on the carbon and nitrogen cycles and the concept of succession. You have already learnt that living organisms require several chemical  elements for their life
process. They may be used as part of their structural  component or as parts of enzymes which
influence various life processes. Nutrients are continuously  exchanged between the organism
and their physical environment.

The  cyclic  movement  of  inorganic  nutrients  or  essential  elements  between  abiotic
environment and biotic communities is called biogeochemical cycle.

Biogeochemical</p><hr><h2>Ecological Adaptations — Hydrophytes and Xerophytes</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Detailed notes on 04 ecological adaptation concept of adaptation hydrophytes and xerophytes within the ecology unit.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Ecological adaptation: the structural, physiological, and behavioural features that allow an organism to survive in a particular environment.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Adaptation types: structural (body form), physiological (internal processes), behavioural (activities).</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Hydrophytes (water plants): adapted to aquatic life; examples: water hyacinth (Nymphaea lotus), lotus, Vallisneria.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Hydrophyte adaptations: aerenchyma (air spaces in tissues for gas exchange), floating leaves, reduced root system, stomata on upper surface, no cuticle or thin cuticle.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Xerophytes (dry-land plants): adapted to arid conditions; examples: cacti, Casuarina, Prosopis.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Xerophyte adaptations: thick cuticle, reduced leaves (spines in cacti), deep or extensive root system, succulent stem, CAM photosynthesis, stomata in pits.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Mesophytes: plants of moderate moisture (most temperate plants); intermediate adaptations.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>NEB focus: concept of adaptation, hydrophytes, and xerophytes with their adaptations.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Adaptation is the result of natural selection over generations; it is not "intentional" or "goal-directed".</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Any factor, abiotic or biotic, that influences the living beings are called as ecological factors or environmental factors.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>These factors either singly or in combination influence or determine the presence or absence, vigour or weakness and relative success or failure of various plant communities in a particular habitat.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Ecological factors are classified into two groups: 4.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>1 ECOSYTEM ECOLOGY 1.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Abiotic Factors: The non-living things of the environment are known as abiotic factors.</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>The abiotic factors that influence the living beings are grouped into three main group: climatic factors, topographic factor and Edaphic factor.</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>Climatic factors : It includes: i.</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>Sunlight – It plays the most important role in an ecosystem.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Ecological adaptation</td></tr><tr><td>• Adaptation</td></tr><tr><td>• Hydrophytes</td></tr><tr><td>• Xerophytes</td></tr><tr><td>Adaptation: structural, physiological, behavioural features for survival.</td></tr><tr><td>Hydrophytes: aerenchyma, floating leaves, reduced roots, thin cuticle.</td></tr><tr><td>Xerophytes: thick cuticle, spines (modified leaves), succulent stem, deep roots, CAM photosynthesis.</td></tr><tr><td>Mesophytes: moderate moisture; intermediate adaptations.</td></tr><tr><td>Adaptation is evolutionary (population level), not individual (acclimatisation).</td></tr><tr><td>Hydrophytes: gas exchange via aerenchyma; Xerophytes: water conservation.</td></tr><tr><td>CAM: open stomata at night, close by day.</td></tr><tr><td>Any factor, abiotic or biotic, that influences the living beings are called as ecological factors or environmental factors.</td></tr><tr><td>These factors either singly or in combination influence or determine the presence or absence, vigour or weakness and relative success or failure of various plant communities in a particular habitat.</td></tr><tr><td>Ecological factors are classified into two groups: 4.</td></tr><tr><td>1 ECOSYTEM ECOLOGY 1.</td></tr><tr><td>Abiotic Factors: The non-living things of the environment are known as abiotic factors.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ Misconception about 04 ecological adaptation concept of adaptation hydrophytes and xerophytes. ✅ Scientific explanation.</td></tr><tr><td>Hydrophytes have AERENCHYMA (air spaces) for gas exchange; xerophytes have reduced surface area to conserve water.</td></tr><tr><td>Cacti's "spines" are modified LEAVES (to reduce water loss); the stem is the photosynthetic organ (succulent).</td></tr><tr><td>Xerophytes often use CAM photosynthesis (open stomata at night to reduce water loss); hydrophytes use the C3 pathway with stomata on the upper surface.</td></tr><tr><td>Hydrophytes may have REDUCED roots (they float; roots are for anchorage, not water uptake).</td></tr><tr><td>Adaptation is a POPULATION-LEVEL feature (evolution over generations), NOT an individual changing during its life.</td></tr><tr><td>Acclimatisation (short-term, individual adjustment) is different from adaptation (long-term, evolutionary).</td></tr><tr><td>Distinguish between key concepts in Ecological Adaptation.</td></tr><tr><td>Understand the differences between similar topics.</td></tr><tr><td>Practice identifying the correct application of principles.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Ecological adaptation refers to the structural, physiological, and behavioural features that allow an organism to survive in its environment. Hydrophytes (water plants) have aerenchyma, floating leaves, reduced roots, and thin cuticles for aquatic life. Xerophytes (dry-land plants) have thick cuticles, spines (modified leaves), succulent stems, deep roots, and CAM photosynthesis for arid conditions. Mesophytes of moderate moisture have intermediate adaptations. Adaptation is an evolutionary (population-level) process, distinct from short-term acclimatisation. NEB focuses on the concept of adaptation, hydrophytes, and xerophytes with their specific adaptations.</p><hr><h2>Ecological Adaptation</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Ecological adaptation: the structural, physiological, and behavioural features that allow an organism to survive in a particular environment.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Adaptation types: structural (body form), physiological (internal processes), behavioural (activities).</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Hydrophytes (water plants): adapted to aquatic life; examples: water hyacinth (Nymphaea lotus), lotus, Vallisneria.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Hydrophyte adaptations: aerenchyma (air spaces in tissues for gas exchange), floating leaves, reduced root system, stomata on upper surface, no cuticle or thin cuticle.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Xerophytes (dry-land plants): adapted to arid conditions; examples: cacti, Casuarina, Prosopis.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Xerophyte adaptations: thick cuticle, reduced leaves (spines in cacti), deep or extensive root system, succulent stem, CAM photosynthesis, stomata in pits.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Mesophytes: plants of moderate moisture (most temperate plants); intermediate adaptations.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>NEB focus: concept of adaptation, hydrophytes, and xerophytes with their adaptations.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Adaptation is the result of natural selection over generations; it is not "intentional" or "goal-directed".</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Adaptation: structural, physiological, behavioural features for survival.</td></tr><tr><td>Hydrophytes: aerenchyma, floating leaves, reduced roots, thin cuticle.</td></tr><tr><td>Xerophytes: thick cuticle, spines (modified leaves), succulent stem, deep roots, CAM photosynthesis.</td></tr><tr><td>Mesophytes: moderate moisture; intermediate adaptations.</td></tr><tr><td>Adaptation is evolutionary (population level), not individual (acclimatisation).</td></tr><tr><td>Hydrophytes: gas exchange via aerenchyma; Xerophytes: water conservation.</td></tr><tr><td>CAM: open stomata at night, close by day.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Hydrophytes have AERENCHYMA (air spaces) for gas exchange; xerophytes have reduced surface area to conserve water.</td></tr><tr><td>Cacti's "spines" are modified LEAVES (to reduce water loss); the stem is the photosynthetic organ (succulent).</td></tr><tr><td>Xerophytes often use CAM photosynthesis (open stomata at night to reduce water loss); hydrophytes use the C3 pathway with stomata on the upper surface.</td></tr><tr><td>Hydrophytes may have REDUCED roots (they float; roots are for anchorage, not water uptake).</td></tr><tr><td>Adaptation is a POPULATION-LEVEL feature (evolution over generations), NOT an individual changing during its life.</td></tr><tr><td>Acclimatisation (short-term, individual adjustment) is different from adaptation (long-term, evolutionary).</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Ecological adaptation refers to the structural, physiological, and behavioural features that allow an organism to survive in its environment. Hydrophytes (water plants) have aerenchyma, floating leaves, reduced roots, and thin cuticles for aquatic life. Xerophytes (dry-land plants) have thick cuticles, spines (modified leaves), succulent stems, deep roots, and CAM photosynthesis for arid conditions. Mesophytes of moderate moisture have intermediate adaptations. Adaptation is an evolutionary (population-level) process, distinct from short-term acclimatisation. NEB focuses on the concept of adaptation, hydrophytes, and xerophytes with their specific adaptations.</p><hr><h2>Ecological Imbalances — Pollution and Climate Change</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Ecological imbalances: human-induced disruptions of natural ecosystems.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Greenhouse effect and climate change: greenhouse gases (CO2, CH4, N2O, CFCs) trap heat → global warming; effects: melting ice, rising sea levels, extreme weather, habitat loss.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Ozone layer depletion: CFCs (chlorofluorocarbons) destroy stratospheric ozone (O3) → increased UV radiation → skin cancer, cataracts, crop damage.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Acid rain: SO2 and NOx from industry + vehicles → H2SO4, HNO3 in rain → acidic rain (pH < 5.6) → damage to forests, aquatic ecosystems, buildings.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Biological invasion: non-native species introduced to new environments → outcompete native species → biodiversity loss; e.g., water hyacinth in Nepal.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Causes: deforestation, industrialisation, urbanisation, agriculture, overpopulation, pollution.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Effects: loss of biodiversity, ecosystem degradation, climate change, health problems.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Control measures: reduce emissions, plant trees, sustainable agriculture, international agreements (Kyoto, Paris), conservation of protected areas.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Nepal: vulnerable to climate change (Himalayan glaciers melting, flooding in Terai); air pollution in Kathmandu Valley (PM2.5 exceeds WHO limits).</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>NEB focus: greenhouse effect and climate change, ozone layer depletion, acid rain, and biological invasion.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>In an ecosystem one can observe the transfer or flow of energy from one trophic level to other trophic level by the process of eating and being eaten.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>It forms a linear chain called food chain.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>There are basically three types of food chain in nature: 1.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Grazing food chain: The food chain that starts from the green plants and ends to carnivores (top consumer) by passing though herbivores.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Detritus food chain: The organic wastes and dead matter derived from grazing food chain are called as detritus.</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>The food chain that starts with detritus is called detritus food chain.</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>Parasitic food chain: This food chain starts either from producers or consumers.</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>It goes from large organisms to smaller ones without killing.</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>Detailed notes on 05 ecological imbalances greenhouse effects and climate change depletion of ozone layer acid rain and biological invasion within the ecology unit.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Greenhouse effect: GHGs trap heat → climate change (natural + enhanced).</td></tr><tr><td>Ozone depletion: CFCs destroy stratospheric O3 → increased UV → skin cancer.</td></tr><tr><td>Acid rain: SO2 + NOx → H2SO4, HNO3 → pH < 5.6 → damage to forests, water, buildings.</td></tr><tr><td>Biological invasion: non-native species outcompete natives → biodiversity loss.</td></tr><tr><td>Causes: deforestation, industry, urbanisation, overpopulation, pollution.</td></tr><tr><td>Effects: biodiversity loss, ecosystem degradation, climate change, health problems.</td></tr><tr><td>Control: reduce emissions, plant trees, sustainable agriculture, international agreements.</td></tr><tr><td>In an ecosystem one can observe the transfer or flow of energy from one trophic level to other trophic level by the process of eating and being eaten.</td></tr><tr><td>It forms a linear chain called food chain.</td></tr><tr><td>There are basically three types of food chain in nature: 1.</td></tr><tr><td>Grazing food chain: The food chain that starts from the green plants and ends to carnivores (top consumer) by passing though herbivores.</td></tr><tr><td>Detritus food chain: The organic wastes and dead matter derived from grazing food chain are called as detritus.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>The greenhouse effect is a NATURAL phenomenon (essential for life); the "enhanced" greenhouse effect (due to human emissions) causes climate change.</td></tr><tr><td>Ozone layer depletion (stratospheric O3 destroyed by CFCs) is different from the greenhouse effect (tropospheric gases trapping heat).</td></tr><tr><td>Acid rain is caused by SO2 and NOx (industry, vehicles), NOT by CO2 alone.</td></tr><tr><td>Biological invasion involves NON-NATIVE species; it is different from natural colonisation.</td></tr><tr><td>Climate change affects ecosystems at all scales (local to global).</td></tr><tr><td>Ozone (O3) is beneficial in the stratosphere (blocks UV) but harmful at ground level (air pollutant, smog).</td></tr><tr><td>Distinguish between key concepts in Food Chain and Food Web.</td></tr><tr><td>Understand the differences between similar topics.</td></tr><tr><td>Practice identifying the correct application of principles.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Misconception about 05 ecological imbalances greenhouse effects and climate change depletion of ozone layer acid rain and biological invasion. ✅ Scientific explanation.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Human activities cause ecological imbalances: the enhanced greenhouse effect (CO2, CH4, CFCs) drives climate change; CFCs deplete the ozone layer, increasing UV radiation; SO2 and NOx cause acid rain (pH < 5.6); and biological invasions (e.g., water hyacinth in Nepal) threaten native biodiversity. These imbalances cause biodiversity loss, ecosystem degradation, and health problems. Control measures include reducing emissions, planting trees, sustainable agriculture, and international agreements (Kyoto, Paris). Nepal is particularly vulnerable to climate change (melting glaciers, Terai flooding) and air pollution (Kathmandu Valley PM2.5). In an ecosystem one can observe the transfer or flow of energy from one trophic level to other
trophic level by the process of eating and being eaten. It forms a linear chain called food chain.
There are basically three types of food chain in nature:
1. Grazing food chain:
The food chain  that  starts  from  the green  plants  and ends  to carnivores  (top  consumer)  by
passing though herbivores.

Figure: 4.1.3 Grazing food chain
2. Detritus food chain:
The organic wastes and dead Summary of 05 ecological imbalances greenhouse effects and climate change depletion of ozone layer acid rain and biological invasion. Human activities cause ecological imbalances: the enhanced greenhouse effect (CO2, CH4, CFCs) drives climate change; CFCs deplete the ozone layer, increasing UV radiation; SO2 and NOx cause acid rain (pH < 5.6); and biological invasions (e.g., water hyacinth in Nepal) threaten native biodiversity. These imbalances cause biodiversity loss, ecosystem degradation, and health problems. Control measures include reducing emissions, planting trees, sustainable agriculture, and international agreements (Kyoto, Paris). Nepal is particularly vulnerable to climate change (melting glaciers, Terai flooding) and air pollution (Kathmandu Valley PM2.5).</p><hr><h2>Ecological Imbalances</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Ecological imbalances: human-induced disruptions of natural ecosystems.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Greenhouse effect and climate change: greenhouse gases (CO2, CH4, N2O, CFCs) trap heat → global warming; effects: melting ice, rising sea levels, extreme weather, habitat loss.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Ozone layer depletion: CFCs (chlorofluorocarbons) destroy stratospheric ozone (O3) → increased UV radiation → skin cancer, cataracts, crop damage.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Acid rain: SO2 and NOx from industry + vehicles → H2SO4, HNO3 in rain → acidic rain (pH < 5.6) → damage to forests, aquatic ecosystems, buildings.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Biological invasion: non-native species introduced to new environments → outcompete native species → biodiversity loss; e.g., water hyacinth in Nepal.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Causes: deforestation, industrialisation, urbanisation, agriculture, overpopulation, pollution.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Effects: loss of biodiversity, ecosystem degradation, climate change, health problems.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Control measures: reduce emissions, plant trees, sustainable agriculture, international agreements (Kyoto, Paris), conservation of protected areas.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Nepal: vulnerable to climate change (Himalayan glaciers melting, flooding in Terai); air pollution in Kathmandu Valley (PM2.5 exceeds WHO limits).</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>NEB focus: greenhouse effect and climate change, ozone layer depletion, acid rain, and biological invasion.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Greenhouse effect: GHGs trap heat → climate change (natural + enhanced).</td></tr><tr><td>Ozone depletion: CFCs destroy stratospheric O3 → increased UV → skin cancer.</td></tr><tr><td>Acid rain: SO2 + NOx → H2SO4, HNO3 → pH < 5.6 → damage to forests, water, buildings.</td></tr><tr><td>Biological invasion: non-native species outcompete natives → biodiversity loss.</td></tr><tr><td>Causes: deforestation, industry, urbanisation, overpopulation, pollution.</td></tr><tr><td>Effects: biodiversity loss, ecosystem degradation, climate change, health problems.</td></tr><tr><td>Control: reduce emissions, plant trees, sustainable agriculture, international agreements.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>The greenhouse effect is a NATURAL phenomenon (essential for life); the "enhanced" greenhouse effect (due to human emissions) causes climate change.</td></tr><tr><td>Ozone layer depletion (stratospheric O3 destroyed by CFCs) is different from the greenhouse effect (tropospheric gases trapping heat).</td></tr><tr><td>Acid rain is caused by SO2 and NOx (industry, vehicles), NOT by CO2 alone.</td></tr><tr><td>Biological invasion involves NON-NATIVE species; it is different from natural colonisation.</td></tr><tr><td>Climate change affects ecosystems at all scales (local to global).</td></tr><tr><td>Ozone (O3) is beneficial in the stratosphere (blocks UV) but harmful at ground level (air pollutant, smog).</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Human activities cause ecological imbalances: the enhanced greenhouse effect (CO2, CH4, CFCs) drives climate change; CFCs deplete the ozone layer, increasing UV radiation; SO2 and NOx cause acid rain (pH < 5.6); and biological invasions (e.g., water hyacinth in Nepal) threaten native biodiversity. These imbalances cause biodiversity loss, ecosystem degradation, and health problems. Control measures include reducing emissions, planting trees, sustainable agriculture, and international agreements (Kyoto, Paris). Nepal is particularly vulnerable to climate change (melting glaciers, Terai flooding) and air pollution (Kathmandu Valley PM2.5).</p><hr><h2>Ecological imbalances: Greenhouse effects and climate cha…</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Scope.** Ecological imbalances: Greenhouse effects and climate cha… — Ecology, Biology (class-11-notes).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Tested.** define it · list: ecological imbalances, greenhouse effects, climate change, depletion of ozone layer · one worked example · one misconception each.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Greenhouse effects.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Climate change.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Depletion of ozone layer.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Acid rain.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Biological invasion.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Ecological imbalances</td></tr><tr><td>• Greenhouse effects</td></tr><tr><td>• Climate change</td></tr><tr><td>• Depletion of ozone layer</td></tr><tr><td>• Acid rain</td></tr><tr><td>• Biological invasion</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ treat "Ecological imbalances" as one blob → ✅ split into: ecological imbalances, greenhouse effects, climate change.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ state the rule without its limit → ✅ note when it applies / stops applying.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ no diagram → ✅ add one labelled figure / structured list.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Ecological imbalances: Greenhouse effects and climate cha… (Ecology, Biology, class-11-notes) covers Ecological imbalances, Greenhouse effects, climate change. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Comparison Tables</h2><h3>Concept Comparison</h3><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td>1: Ecology — Concepts and Ecosystems</td><td>Summary of 01 ecosystem ecology concept of ecology biotic and abiotic factors species interactions concept of ecosystem structural and functional aspects of pond and forest ecosystem. Ecology studies the interactions between organisms and their environment. Biotic (living) and abiotic (non-living) factors shape every ecosystem. Species interact through mutualism, commensalism, parasitism, predation, and competition. An ecosystem is a functional unit of biotic and abiotic components; its structural aspects (producers, consumers, decomposers, trophic levels) and functional aspects (unidirectional energy flow + cyclic nutrient cycling) define how it operates. Pond and forest ecosystems (e.g., Chitwan Terai, temperate hills) illustrate these principles in the Nepali context. In nature an organism does not exist in isolation,  it is associated with organisms of its
own and of different  kinds as well as with the physical environment. If the organism is plant,
it depends for growth on the aerial environment  for light, temperature and carbon dioxide, on
the soil environment for inorganic nutrients and water. If it is an animal it depends on the plants
for food and shelter. If it is a microorganism  it depend on the dead organic matter from plants
and animals  for Ecology studies the interactions between organisms and their environment. Biotic (living) and abiotic (non-living) factors shape every ecosystem. Species interact through mutualism, commensalism, parasitism, predation, and competition. An ecosystem is a functional unit of biotic and abiotic components; its structural aspects (producers, consumers, decomposers, trophic levels) and functional aspects (unidirectional energy flow + cyclic nutrient cycling) define how it operates. Pond and forest ecosystems (e.g., Chitwan Terai, temperate hills) illustrate these principles in the Nepali context.</td></tr><tr><td>2: Ecosystem ecology: Concept of ecology, biotic and abiotic…</td><td>Ecosystem ecology: Concept of ecology, biotic and abiotic… (Ecology, Biology, class-11-notes) covers Ecosystem ecology, ecology, biotic. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>3: Ecosystem Ecology</td><td>Ecology studies the interactions between organisms and their environment. Biotic (living) and abiotic (non-living) factors shape every ecosystem. Species interact through mutualism, commensalism, parasitism, predation, and competition. An ecosystem is a functional unit of biotic and abiotic components; its structural aspects (producers, consumers, decomposers, trophic levels) and functional aspects (unidirectional energy flow + cyclic nutrient cycling) define how it operates. Pond and forest ecosystems (e.g., Chitwan Terai, temperate hills) illustrate these principles in the Nepali context.</td></tr><tr><td>4: Concept of ecosystem, structural and functional aspects o…</td><td>Concept of ecosystem, structural and functional aspects o… (Ecology, Biology, class-11-notes) covers ecosystem, structural, functional aspects of pond. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>5: Food Chain, Food Web and Ecological Pyramids</td><td>Summary of 02 food chain food web trophic level ecological pyramids productivity. Food chain, food web, trophic level, ecological pyramids,… (Ecology, Biology, class-11-notes) covers Food chain, food web, trophic level. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these. Energy and matter flow through ecosystems in food chains (linear) and food webs (networks). Trophic levels (producers → primary consumers → secondary → tertiary) structure these. Ecological pyramids (number, biomass, energy) visualise trophic structure; the pyramid of energy is always upright because ~90% of energy is lost at each transfer (10% rule). Productivity (GPP, NPP) measures the rate of energy capture and availability to consumers. NEB focuses on food chains, food webs, trophic levels, the three pyramids, and productivity calculations. In an ecosystem one can observe the transfer or flow of energy from one trophic level to other
trophic level by the process of eating and being eaten. It forms a linear chain called food chain.
There are basically three types of food chain in nature:
1. Grazing food chain:
The food chain  that  starts  from  the green  plants  and ends  to carnivores  (top  consumer)  by
passing though herbivores.

Figure: 4.1.3 Grazing food chain
