The organic wastes and dead</td></tr><tr><td>6: Food Chain and Food Web</td><td>Energy and matter flow through ecosystems in food chains (linear) and food webs (networks). Trophic levels (producers → primary consumers → secondary → tertiary) structure these. Ecological pyramids (number, biomass, energy) visualise trophic structure; the pyramid of energy is always upright because ~90% of energy is lost at each transfer (10% rule). Productivity (GPP, NPP) measures the rate of energy capture and availability to consumers. NEB focuses on food chains, food webs, trophic levels, the three pyramids, and productivity calculations.</td></tr><tr><td>7: Biogeochemical Cycles</td><td>Biogeochemical cycles move essential elements through living and non-living compartments. The carbon cycle (gaseous CO2) and the nitrogen cycle (N2 → NH3 → NO2- → NO3- → N2) are the most important; bacteria (Rhizobium, Nitrosomonas, Pseudomonas) drive key steps. The phosphorus cycle has no gaseous phase and is the slowest. Succession is the directional change of an ecosystem toward a stable climax community: primary succession starts on bare rock, secondary on existing soil. NEB focuses on the carbon and nitrogen cycles and the concept of succession.</td></tr><tr><td>8: Biogeochemical cycles: carbon and nitrogen cycles; concep…</td><td>Biogeochemical cycles: carbon and nitrogen cycles; concep… (Ecology, Biology, class-11-notes) covers Biogeochemical cycles, carbon, nitrogen cycles. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these. Summary of 03 biogeochemical cycles carbon and nitrogen cycles concept of succession. Biogeochemical cycles move essential elements through living and non-living compartments. The carbon cycle (gaseous CO2) and the nitrogen cycle (N2 → NH3 → NO2- → NO3- → N2) are the most important; bacteria (Rhizobium, Nitrosomonas, Pseudomonas) drive key steps. The phosphorus cycle has no gaseous phase and is the slowest. Succession is the directional change of an ecosystem toward a stable climax community: primary succession starts on bare rock, secondary on existing soil. NEB focuses on the carbon and nitrogen cycles and the concept of succession. You have already learnt that living organisms require several chemical  elements for their life
process. They may be used as part of their structural  component or as parts of enzymes which
influence various life processes. Nutrients are continuously  exchanged between the organism
and their physical environment.

The  cyclic  movement  of  inorganic  nutrients  or  essential  elements  between  abiotic
environment and biotic communities is called biogeochemical cycle.

Biogeochemical</td></tr><tr><td>9: Ecological Adaptations — Hydrophytes and Xerophytes</td><td>Ecological adaptation refers to the structural, physiological, and behavioural features that allow an organism to survive in its environment. Hydrophytes (water plants) have aerenchyma, floating leaves, reduced roots, and thin cuticles for aquatic life. Xerophytes (dry-land plants) have thick cuticles, spines (modified leaves), succulent stems, deep roots, and CAM photosynthesis for arid conditions. Mesophytes of moderate moisture have intermediate adaptations. Adaptation is an evolutionary (population-level) process, distinct from short-term acclimatisation. NEB focuses on the concept of adaptation, hydrophytes, and xerophytes with their specific adaptations.</td></tr><tr><td>10: Ecological Adaptation</td><td>Ecological adaptation refers to the structural, physiological, and behavioural features that allow an organism to survive in its environment. Hydrophytes (water plants) have aerenchyma, floating leaves, reduced roots, and thin cuticles for aquatic life. Xerophytes (dry-land plants) have thick cuticles, spines (modified leaves), succulent stems, deep roots, and CAM photosynthesis for arid conditions. Mesophytes of moderate moisture have intermediate adaptations. Adaptation is an evolutionary (population-level) process, distinct from short-term acclimatisation. NEB focuses on the concept of adaptation, hydrophytes, and xerophytes with their specific adaptations.</td></tr><tr><td>11: Ecological Imbalances — Pollution and Climate Change</td><td>Human activities cause ecological imbalances: the enhanced greenhouse effect (CO2, CH4, CFCs) drives climate change; CFCs deplete the ozone layer, increasing UV radiation; SO2 and NOx cause acid rain (pH < 5.6); and biological invasions (e.g., water hyacinth in Nepal) threaten native biodiversity. These imbalances cause biodiversity loss, ecosystem degradation, and health problems. Control measures include reducing emissions, planting trees, sustainable agriculture, and international agreements (Kyoto, Paris). Nepal is particularly vulnerable to climate change (melting glaciers, Terai flooding) and air pollution (Kathmandu Valley PM2.5). In an ecosystem one can observe the transfer or flow of energy from one trophic level to other
trophic level by the process of eating and being eaten. It forms a linear chain called food chain.
There are basically three types of food chain in nature:
1. Grazing food chain:
The food chain  that  starts  from  the green  plants  and ends  to carnivores  (top  consumer)  by
passing though herbivores.

Figure: 4.1.3 Grazing food chain
2. Detritus food chain:
